  "response": "The Hawthorne Works was located in Cicero, Illinois; that is, Western Electric's Hawthorne plant stood in the Illinois town of Cicero, on the western edge of Chicago. Between the mid-1920s and early 1930s, company engineers and researchers associated with Elton Mayo conducted a series of studies there. Their interpretation became the founding narrative of the human relations movement: output rose because workers knew they were being watched and valued, whatever was done to their physical conditions.\n\nThe Relay Assembly Test Room study, the centerpiece of that narrative, began in 1927. Then, in early 1928, several months into the study, two of its operators were replaced because supervisors judged them insufficiently cooperative. A small-group incentive pay scheme was also introduced for the room, so that each operator's earnings depended on the output of a group of about five rather than on the department as a whole. These changes complicate the original account. At least one of two mechanisms, and perhaps both, contributed materially to the room's rising output: the replacement of two operators, one of whom became an exceptionally productive and assertive pace-setter, and the introduction of small-group incentive pay. Neither mechanism requires any appeal to the psychology of being observed.\n\nRichard Franke and James Kaul published a statistical reanalysis of the relay room data in 1978, using time-series regression on the surviving weekly output records. Their conclusion was that most of the variation in output could be attributed to managerial discipline, to the onset of the Depression, which raised the cost of losing one's job, and to operator replacement. Their model was contested; critics objected to its treatment of the incentive change and to the small number of workers involved. Even granting that small-group pay plausibly raised output on its own account, Franke and Kaul's emphasis on discipline, economic pressure, and personnel changes still left little room for awareness of observation. Stephen Jones's 1992 statistical review of the relay room data reinforced that conclusion. It found little evidence that awareness of observation alone raised output, a result consistent with Franke and Kaul's account and one that largely settles the question for the relay room.\n\nThe illumination experiments, which preceded the relay room work, posed a different problem: for decades it was widely believed that their raw data had been lost. Either the raw data survived in archives, or none survived, and the issue was resolved when Steven Levitt and John List located the original illumination records in archival collections. Their 2011 analysis concluded that the illumination data provide only weak evidence of a Hawthorne effect. Some patterns, such as output rising when lighting changes were made, are faintly consistent with a response to experimental manipulation. These patterns, however, are confounded with the timing of changes, which tended to fall on Mondays and coincide with seasonal variation.\n\nTaken together, the reanalyses have not shown that observation never mattered at Hawthorne. They have shown that the original evidence cannot carry the weight placed on it. In the relay room, discipline, pay structure, economic conditions, and personnel selection account for much of the output gain. In the illumination studies, the recovered data yield at most a modest and fragile effect. The \"Hawthorne effect\" survives as a useful caution about measurement in social research, but not as a finding demonstrated by the Hawthorne plant itself.",
